## Code Conventions

### Imports

```

* React/hooks first
* Types next
* Services and utilities
* Components (static first, lazy-loaded last)

```

### Naming
- **Components:** PascalCase (e.g., `Level1_Standard.tsx`)
- **Services:** camelCase (e.g., `audioService.ts`, `csvUtils.ts`)
- **Types/Interfaces:** PascalCase (e.g., `CSVRow`, `TileData`)
- **Enums:** PascalCase (e.g., `GameMode`)
- **Constants:** UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `HEADER_MIN_HEIGHT`)

### Styling
- **Tailwind classes** used via CDN. Do not add custom CSS unless necessary.
- **Neon color palette** is defined in `index.html` tailwind config and `types.ts`.
- Use `bg-neon-*`, `shadow-[0_0_15px_#COLOR,...]` patterns for glowing effects.
- **Always use the existing neon color scheme.** Never introduce new color systems.
- Never change the font (Oswald) without explicit instruction.
- **Strict Visual Hard Borders:** Do not use soft, blurred, or diffused shadows/borders on container boxes, panels, or custom layouts. Always prioritize crisp outline strokes, solid inline fills, and high-contrast bounding configurations to align cleanly with the retro arcade aesthetic.

### CSV Data Format

```

id,Category Name,"Description",Word1,"Definition1",Word2,"Definition2",...,Difficulty,BroadCategory

```
- Each row: id | name | description | (word + definition) pairs | difficulty | broadCategory
- Words: alternating word-definition pairs after the description; words MUST NOT exceed a **13 character maximum limit per line	**.
- Difficulty: numeric (1=easy, 3=medium, 5=hard)
- BroadCategory: one of Nature, Animals, Astronomy, Food, History, Geography, Religion, Technology, Science, Music, Literature, Movies, Television, Economics, Politics, Lifestyle, Gaming, Cars, Sports, Art, Mythology

### Game Modes (from GameMode enum)
- `CLASSIC` - Group 4 related word tiles. Uses `csvPoolData*.ts` for game data
- `LEVEL_EMOJI` - Emoji tiles instead of words. Uses `emojiData.ts` for game data
- `LEVEL_MIND_MATCH` - Timed matching rounds (20 tiles, 4 per match, 3 rounds, 3 errors). Uses `csvPoolData*.ts` for game data
- `LEVEL_SYNONYMS` - Group by meaning. Uses `CSV_SYNONYMS_*.ts` for game data
- `LEVEL_THEMED` - Themed categories. Uses `csvThemeData*.ts` for game data
- `LEVEL_EXPANSION` / `LEVEL_CASCADE` - Uses `csvPoolData*.ts` for game data
- `LEVEL_FILTER` - Filter mode. Uses `csvPoolData*.ts` for game data
- `LEVEL_GROUP` - Group mode. Uses `csvPoolData*.ts` for game data
- `LEVEL_HIDDEN` - Tiles start hidden, revealed when row solved. Uses `csvPoolData*.ts` for game data

---

## Button & Interaction Animations

### Next Level Button
- Located in `Header.tsx`, rendered during review state when `isReviewing && onNext` are truthy
- Background color cycles through `NEON_CYCLE_COLORS` array every 1500ms via `useEffect` interval
- Border uses `animate-border-pulse` CSS animation (white glow pulse)
- Active press: `active:scale-95`
- On click: Calls `onNext` → `proceedToNextLevel()` in App.tsx which advances levelIndex, saves progress, shows interstitial ad

### Start Game Button
- Located in `LevelMenu.tsx`, rendered on main menu
- Hover: `group hover:scale-105` with `transition-transform duration-300`
- Active press: `active:scale-95` + `active:translate-y-[2px]`
- On click: `onStart` → navigates to HowToPlay (first time) or game mode

### Hint Lightbulb Button
- Located in `Header.tsx`, rendered in header top-right when `onHintClick && hintCount !== undefined`
- Active press: `active:scale-90`
- Three visual states: Active (yellow `#F9FF00`), Depleted (dimmed `#444444` with glow pulse), Blocked (grey `#555555`)
- Badge shows remaining hint count
- On click: Triggers `handleHintClick` in App.tsx → dispatches `'hint-used'` CustomEvent or shows `HintAdOverlay`
- Hint sound effect: `audio.playHint()` — bright ascending chime (procedural Web Audio API)

---

## Sound Effects

All sound effects are synthesized procedurally using the Web Audio API (`audioService.ts`). No audio files are loaded.

| Method | Description | Used By |
|--------|-------------|---------|
| `playTick()` | Short square wave beep | General interaction feedback |
| `playSelect()` | Sine tone at 850Hz | Tile selection |
| `playSwap()` | Frequency sweep 350→700Hz | Tile swap animation |
| `playCorrect()` | Two ascending sine tones (660Hz + 880Hz) | Correct match |
| `playRowSolved()` | Three-note ascending chord (440, 554, 659Hz) | Row completion |
| `playError()` | Descending sawtooth buzz (120→60Hz) | Wrong match |
| `playWin()` | Ascending fanfare (523, 659, 784, 1046Hz) | Level completion |
| `playLevelStart()` | Two-tone startup (440Hz + 880Hz) | Level start |
| `playStar()` | Bright shimmering chime | Star earned in review |
| `playHint()` | Ascending sparkle chime (1046, 1318, 1567, 2093Hz) | Hint usage confirmation |

---

## Accessibility Requirements

### Touch Target Size
- All interactive elements (buttons, toggles, icon buttons, clickable tiles) MUST have a minimum touch target of 48dp × 48dp.
- Use Tailwind classes `min-w-[48px] min-h-[48px]` on buttons to ensure this.
- Icon-only buttons (close/X, hamburger menu, etc.) must also meet this requirement.
- If a button's visual size is smaller than 48dp, add padding or use `min-w-[48px] min-h-[48px]` with `flex items-center justify-center`.

### Content Description
- All interactive non-TextView elements MUST have a `contentDescription` or `aria-label` attribute for screen reader accessibility.
- Icon-only buttons MUST have a descriptive `aria-label` explaining the action (e.g., `"Close settings"`, `"Open settings menu"`).
- Images MUST have meaningful `alt` text.
- Game tiles should have descriptive content via their visible word text.

### Visual Contrast
- App text and foreground content must maintain:
  - **3:1 contrast ratio** for large text (18pt+ or 14pt+ bold) and graphics
  - **4.5:1 contrast ratio** for small text (less than 18pt or less than 14pt bold)
- The neon color scheme on black background generally provides strong contrast.
- Avoid pairing low-contrast colors (e.g., light gray on white, dark gray on black) for text readability.
- Text on `bg-zinc-900` or `bg-black` backgrounds should use white or bright neon colors for sufficient contrast.

